{# What people are saying, beside a grid of drawings: Home's feeds, each
   with the comments of its own people or places (handlers/home.rs,
   Feed::comment_scope), and a community's drawings. `comments` is a first
   batch from comments_context(); `more_url` is the page the whole list is
   on -- a feed's /comments, a community's.

   One markup for every width; style.css decides how much of it shows. On a
   wide window it is a column beside the grid that holds still as the grid
   scrolls, and scrolls on its own, loading the next batch as it reaches
   the end. Narrower there is no room beside the grid, and a list that
   scrolled inside the page, or kept pushing the drawings down, would be
   worse than none: it is the latest three, in the same rows, and a link on
   to the page with the rest. The feed's section lays it out
   (`.feed-layout`), after the header and before the grid, the order it
   reads in at every width.

   Nothing to show, nothing rendered, and the grid has the width to itself. #}
{% macro comments_aside(comments, more_url) %}
  {% if comments.rows %}
    <aside class="feed-comments" aria-labelledby="feed-comments-title">
      <h2 id="feed-comments-title" class="feed-comments-title">{{ ftl_get_message("recent-comments") }}</h2>
      <div class="feed-comments-panel">
        <div class="feed-comments-list">
          {% include "comments_fragment.jinja" %}
        </div>
        {# Only when there is something past the three a phone shows. #}
        {% if comments.rows|length > 3 or comments.next_url %}
          <a class="feed-comments-more" href="{{ more_url }}">{{ ftl_get_message("recent-comments-more") }}</a>
        {% endif %}
      </div>
    </aside>
  {% endif %}
{% endmacro %}
